                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>The infiltration test revealed air leakage through the followings house components:



- Attic hatch

- The window located on the left back wall on the main floor

- The double kitchen doors

- Receptacles

- The chimney and connections to the propane fireplace

- The old chimney lacated in the basement. The space has not been sealed properly.

- The main floor bathroom fan



We recommend to seal this particular places. Air leakage contribute directly to heat loss and affect the energy consumption to heat the interior space. Also when the air leakage is high the house can become very dry specially during winter time. This situation may cause health problems. </airSeal>
                <mainWalls>The walls are estimated to be made with 2x4 with a level of insulation of R-12. This R value is to low and doesn't meet the actual standard of R-28. We recommend the addition of insulation to meet the standard. If you add a minimum of R-20+ you are eligible to receive a rebate of $2.50/square foot or $3.50/square foot for a minimum of R-30 +.



If you are planning to do so, we suggest to proceed from the outside part of the wall. This technique will give you the opportunity to seal the building properly. Also by working outside you will not reduce the interior space.</mainWalls>
                <ceilingsCathedral>In this moment the insulation level of the roof is of R-22. This level is also under the actual standard of R-60. Perhaps with the structure of this type of roof, it might be not possible to add insulation. </ceilingsCathedral>
                <exposedFloors>We estimate the insulation of the exposed floor to be R-20. During the infiltration test we notice air coming through this section. We recommend to increase the level of insulation to be R-30.</exposedFloors>
                <foundation>At this moment the basement is composed by a concrete wall and a pony wall (section above the concrete foundation wall).

FOUNDATION WALL: 1'' of batt insulation which represent R-3.2

PONY WALL: 3.5'' of batt insulation which represent R-12



We recommend the addition of insulation mustly on the concrete wall to reduce the thermal bridge effet. The thermal bridge effet is the transmission of cold by conduction that comes through the wall. This situation contribute to the feeling of cold in the basement and also to constantly lower the basement temperature. In other therm you lose the heat from the interior space.



Also when the air leakage of the basement is high another phenomen called the chimney effect happen. When the cold air penetrates into the warm side of the house, it pushes the warm air to the top level creating a column of heat going up, The more cold the outside temperature is, the higher this phenomen is.



This is why the insulation of the basement is so important, and so the insulation of the header joist (Joist that run along the perimeter of the house).</foundation>
                <windows>We notice some air leakage from the window, but nothing major at this time. Eventuallly it will be a good idea to replace all windows by ENERGY STAR certified for ZONE 3 windows.</windows>
                <AdditionalComments>The section of the chimney located in the basement has been poorly insulated and sealed. We recommend to re-insulate and seal properly this section.</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
